window.docContents[67].resolve({"/Constructions/#section-complete-multipartite-graphs-and-blow-ups":{"contents":"Having earlier introduced complete bipartite graphs we would now like to discuss\ncomplete multipartite graphs and their generalization blow-up graphs. However these\nconstructions make use of the SimpleGraph.comap construction which we won't\nformally introduce until Comaps. In order to properly understand\nthese constructions you may wish to read that short section now.\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.6. Complete Multipartite Graphs and Blow-ups*","id":"/Constructions/#section-complete-multipartite-graphs-and-blow-ups"},"/Theorems/#chapter-theorems":{"contents":"namespace SimpleGraph\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\nopen Finset\n\n\nThere are quite a few classical theorems about graphs in Mathlib (but many more are\nstill missing!).\n\nIn this chapter we survey the graph-theoretic results that Mathlib formalizes,\nbuilding on the definitions of the earlier chapters. Proofs are mostly left to\nMathlib: we state each result, explain how its statement is phrased in Lean, and\napply it to our running examples. Where a natural generalization is missing from\nMathlib and easy to give we prove it ourselves.\n\nWe begin with matchings: Hall's marriage theorem and its deficiency version, which\ngives Kőnig's theorem, symmetric differences of perfect matchings, and Tutte's\ntheorem. We then turn to extremal graph theory: Turán's theorem (with Mantel's\ntheorem as a special case), Füredi's stability theorem, the Andrásfai–Erdős–Sós\ntheorem, Zykov's theorem on the number of cliques, and the extremal numbers\nextremalNumber n H, together with their asymptotic behaviour (Turán density and\nthe Erdős–Stone theorem). The two final sections are more advanced: Szemerédi's\nregularity lemma and the triangle removal lemma, with Roth's theorem on arithmetic\nprogressions as an application, and two results about infinite graphs, Kőnig's lemma\nand the existence of ends.\n\nMany famous theorems are not (yet) in Mathlib. These include Ramsey's theorem for\ngraphs, the Kővári–Sós–Turán theorem, Kirchhoff's matrix-tree theorem, Cayley's\nformula, Euler's circuit theorem, Euler's formula and planarity, Menger's theorem,\nBrooks' theorem, Vizing's theorem, Berge's theorem, Kőnig's theorem on bipartite\nmatchings and the theorems of Dirac and Ore. (Some of these are proved in this\nguide: Euler's theorem on Eulerian trails, the\ntheorems of Dirac and\nOre, Brooks' theorem and\nKőnig's theorem.)\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean","header":"13. Theorems","id":"/Theorems/#chapter-theorems"},"/Walks/#section-ore-s-condition-and-closing-the-path":{"contents":"We can now assemble the construction. Given a maximal path of length ≥ 2, either the\nendpoints are adjacent (so it can be closed with concat) or the crossing pair gives\na Walk.posaClosed. In both cases we obtain a cycle based at u through exactly the\nvertices of p, of length p.length + 1.\n\nNote that all we need for this is the degree-sum bound for the single pair of\nendpoints, and only when they are non-adjacent. This is Ore's condition at a\npair of vertices, and since applying it one pair at a time will be a recurring theme,\nwe make it a definition: IsOrePair.\n\n/-- *Ore's condition* at a pair of vertices: if u and v are distinct and\nnon-adjacent then their degree sum is at least the number of vertices. (An\nadjacent or equal pair satisfies the condition vacuously.) -/\ndef IsOrePair (G : SimpleGraph V) [Fintype V] (u v : V)\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)] [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] : Prop :=\n  u ≠ v → ¬ G.Adj u v → Fintype.card V ≤ G.degree u + G.degree v\n\n\nThe u ≠ v condition matters when we require Ore's condition of every pair of\nvertices to define an Ore graph below: without it the case u = v would demand\n|V| ≤ 2 * deg u for every vertex, which is Dirac's condition rather than Ore's.\n\nlemma Walk.IsMaximalPath.exists_cycle_spanning [Fintype V] {u v : V}\n    [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)] [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)] {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hmax : p.IsMaximalPath) (h2 : 2 ≤ p.length) (hcard : G.IsOrePair u v) :\n    ∃ c : G.Walk u u, c.IsCycle ∧ c.length = p.length + 1 ∧\n      (∀ x, x ∈ c.support ↔ x ∈ p.support) := by\n  by_cases huv : G.Adj u v\n  · -- Adjacent endpoints: close p with concat.\n    refine ⟨p.concat huv.symm, (concat_isCycle_iff_length_ne_one p huv.symm).mpr\n      ⟨hmax.isPath, (by grind)⟩, length_concat .., fun x => ?_⟩\n    grind [support_concat, start_mem_support]\n  · -- Non-adjacent endpoints: rotate via the crossing pair, close with posaClosed.\n    have huv_ne : u ≠ v := by grind [isPath_iff_nil, hmax.isPath]\n    obtain ⟨i, h₁, h₂⟩ := hmax.exists_crossing_index (hcard huv_ne huv)\n    exact ⟨p.posaClosed h₁ h₂, hmax.isPath.posaClosed_isCycle h2 h₁ h₂,\n      length_posaClosed .., fun x => by simp⟩\n\n\nA Hamiltonian path is trivially maximal, since every vertex lies on it. So a\nHamiltonian path of length at least 2 whose endpoints form an Ore pair closes up into\na cycle through every vertex: a Hamiltonian cycle.\n\nlemma Walk.IsHamiltonian.exists_isHamiltonianCycle_of_isOrePair [Fintype V]\n    [DecidableEq V] {u v : V} [Fintype (G.neighborSet u)] [Fintype (G.neighborSet v)]\n    {p : G.Walk u v}\n    (hp : p.IsHamiltonian) (h2 : 2 ≤ p.length) (hcard : G.IsOrePair u v) :\n    ∃ c : G.Walk u u, c.IsHamiltonianCycle := by\n  have hmax : p.IsMaximalPath :=\n    ⟨hp.isPath, fun w _ => hp.mem_support w, fun w _ => hp.mem_support w⟩\n  obtain ⟨c, hcyc, hclen, -⟩ := hmax.exists_cycle_spanning h2 hcard\n  exact ⟨c, isHamiltonianCycle_iff_isCycle_and_length_eq.2\n    ⟨hcyc, by grind [hp.length_support, Walk.length_support]⟩⟩\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Walks\u0009Hamiltonian Graphs: Ore, Dirac and Bondy–Chvátal*","header":"8.8.3. Ore's Condition and Closing the Path","id":"/Walks/#section-ore-s-condition-and-closing-the-path"}});